1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Hi, in this video we'll look at testing for stored XSS.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,480
Stored XSS arises when an application receives data from an untrusted source and includes

3
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:20,560
that data within its later HTTP responses in an unsafe way.

4
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:26,400
In a stored XSS attack, the attacker places their exploit into the application, but the

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,000
exploit only executes when a user visits a particular location.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
For example, an attacker may place an exploit in a blog post comment that later executes

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,720
when a victim user visits the blog post.

8
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:44,220
While Burp Scanner can detect stored XSS, you can also use Burp to manually identify linked

9
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:48,960
input and output points in the application, then test these links to determine whether

10
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:52,080
a stored XSS vulnerability is present.

11
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:57,460
If you'd like to follow along, you can use the lab, stored XSS into HTML context with

12
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:00,640
nothing encoded from our Web Security Academy.

13
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:05,320
Once the lab has launched, we first need to identify points where user input is stored,

14
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:10,680
and then later displayed by the application, and link these input and output points.

15
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:15,880
To do this, we'll go to Proxy Intercept and click to toggle Intercept on, so Burp intercepts

16
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:20,360
each request and displays the message details in the Intercept tab.

17
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:24,800
Now we can browse around the site and review each request to identify data entry points that

18
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:33,980
may be vulnerable to stored XSS, such as this comment form.

19
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:39,120
We can check we have submitted a unique value into each of the identified data entry points,

20
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:49,560
and then click forward to send the request.

21
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:55,460
We can now go to the Proxy HTTP History tab to filter any messages that include these unique

22
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:56,500
values.

23
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:02,760
Click on the filter bar, under Filter by Search Term, enter the unique value.

24
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:03,760
Click Apply.

25
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:10,320
The HTTP History tab now only shows messages that include the unique value.

26
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:15,400
You can now review the messages in the HTTP History tab to identify any linked input and

27
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,880
output points.

28
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:21,640
Now we can start testing for stored XSS.

29
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:25,380
Once we've found the linked input and output points, we can right click and use Send to

30
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:31,060
Repeater for both the initial request that results in the input being stored, and the later requests

31
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:34,860
that results in the input being displayed.

32
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:38,480
Switch to the Repeater tab.

33
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:42,560
Create a group in Repeater that includes the two messages by right clicking a message and selecting

34
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:46,520
Add Tab to Group, Create Tab Group.

35
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:52,500
Make sure both messages are added to the folder, and click Create.

36
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:57,020
Then make sure the messages are in the correct order so the input message is before the output

37
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:03,220
message.

38
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:09,360
In the input request, change the data entry point to a proof of concept XSS payload.

39
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:15,000
For example, Script, Alert1, Slash Script.

40
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,160
Click the Send drop down menu, then select Send Group in Sequence, Separate Connections.

41
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:27,440
Click Send Group, Separate Connections to send the two requests.

42
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:34,300
Then right click on the output message, and select Show Response in Browser.

43
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:37,460
It displays a dialog containing a URL.

44
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:42,540
Copy and paste this URL into your browser to see if the proof of concept ran successfully.

45
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:46,300
If you used the alert function in your payload, you should see an alert popup.

46
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:51,940
Click the Send drop down menu, then select Show Response in Browser.

47
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:55,940
Click the Send drop down menu, then select Show Response in Browser.

